00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,800
 Welcome to our next chapter, Chapter 5 Life in the Greenhouse here on Earth, where we'll

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:12,300
 also learn about photosynthesis and global climate change.

3
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:18,080
 Make sure you check out our learning outcomes and recognize them as we come to them throughout

4
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,520
 our lessons and readings.

5
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:23,360
 Here's a question.

6
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,200
 Most scientists disagree that global climate change is occurring.

7
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:34,600
 Well, the answer to that one is a big false.

8
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:41,920
 In late 2015, high-level representatives of governments of nearly all the Earth's nations

9
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:45,280
 met in Paris for a historic dialogue.

10
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:53,440
 The end result was an agreement signed on December 12 in 2015 to combat global warming.

11
00:00:53,440 --> 00:01:02,040
 The 195 signatory countries agreed to eliminate human-derived greenhouse gas emissions by 2050.

12
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:07,460
 As the agreement is being implemented, developing countries are to receive financial assistance

13
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:09,760
 to help cut emissions.

14
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:13,920
 And aid will be given to those nations that lack the resources to mitigate the negative

15
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,680
 effects of climate change.

16
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:22,120
 In dozens of cities throughout the world, hundreds of thousands of activists and ordinary

17
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:28,720
 citizens participated in peaceful and dramatic marches in the days leading up to the conference.

18
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:33,400
 The size of these rallies may have given world leaders the confidence to act.

19
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:40,320
 Indeed, many of these leaders were exhilarated by the hard-won final product.

20
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:47,240
 This is truly a historic moment, said Ban Kimun, the United Nations Secretary-General.

21
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:52,080
 For the first time, we've truly a universal agreement on climate change, one of the most

22
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:55,080
 crucial problems on Earth.

23
00:01:55,080 --> 00:02:02,000
 Many environmental activists were more circumspect, noting that the accord did not specify any

24
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,400
 particular actions to combat the growing threat of climate change.

25
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:13,760
 Secretary-General Ban Kimun's description of climate change as one of Earth's most crucial

26
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:16,520
 problems is not an exaggeration.

27
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:23,160
 But what could sound like a small increase, the 1 degree Celsius temperature rise recorded

28
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:29,440
 since the beginning of the Industrial Revolution, dramatic climate shifts have already occurred.

29
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:33,280
 Mountain glaciers and polar ice caps are disappearing.

30
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:39,720
 While sea levels rise, storms have become more severe and destructive, and insects carrying

31
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:44,280
 disease have changed their distribution patterns.

32
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:47,960
 This has threatened human populations.

33
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:55,280
 Scientists say that anything above a 2 degree Celsius, which is 3.6 degrees Fahrenheit, increase

34
00:02:55,280 --> 00:03:02,560
 over the historical average will doom us to a future of even more severe consequences.

35
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:08,360
 These consequences include even more severe storms, and resulting widespread food and

36
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:10,760
 water shortages.

37
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:16,680
 Given what scientists say about the severity of the crisis and the consequences of inaction,

38
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:22,040
 it may be crucial for humanity's survival and well-being that we band together to address

39
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:24,000
 climate change.

40
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,840
 The Paris Accord, although far from perfect, is an important step in that process.

41
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:36,640
 But what can science tell us about how we got in this predicament in the first place?

42
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:47,200
 And perhaps more important, what can we do about it now?

43
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:52,520
 Global warming is the progressive increase of Earth's average temperature that's been

44
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:56,680
 occurring over the past century.

45
00:03:56,680 --> 00:04:02,320
 There's little debate among scientists and government-appointed panels about the nature

46
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:07,680
 and cause of global warming.

47
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:11,720
 And global warming is contributing to global climate change.

48
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:16,960
 The local changes in average temperature, precipitation and sea level relative to historical

49
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:26,240
 conditions that are occurring in locations all over the planet.

50
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:32,680
 Scientists who publish and peer reviewed journals and respected scientific panels and societies

51
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:40,320
 such as the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Control, otherwise known as the IPCC, the

52
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:45,920
 National Academy of Sciences, and the American Association for the Advancement of Science

53
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:52,760
 the AAAS all agree that the average temperature on Earth is increasing and that most of the

54
00:04:52,760 --> 00:05:01,280
 global warming observed in the past century is attributable to human activities.

55
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:09,400
 Again, global climate change is the local changes in average climate conditions, including

56
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:21,960
 temperature, precipitation and sea level, and this is again relative to historic conditions.

57
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:28,040
 Global Earth's climate does fluctuate over time due to changes in Earth's orbit and solar

58
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:30,280
 output.

59
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:38,360
 Human-caused or anthropogenic global warming has dramatically increased the rate of change.

60
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:46,120
 Again, it's about here the rate of change, so much so it might be difficult for humans

61
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:49,720
 to even adjust.

62
00:05:49,720 --> 00:05:55,760
 Economic global warming is caused by recent increases in the concentration of particular

63
00:05:55,760 --> 00:06:00,440
 gases called greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.

64
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:13,520
 These include water vapor, carbon dioxide or CO2, methane known as CH4, and ozone or O3.

65
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:19,680
 The accumulation of many of the greenhouse gases is a direct result of coal, oil, and

66
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:22,920
 natural gas combustion.

67
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:30,200
 The most abundant gas emitted by the combustion of fossil fuels is carbon dioxide, so for

68
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:39,600
 this reason carbon dioxide is considered the most important greenhouse gas to control.

69
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:45,360
 Much atmospheric gas is a greenhouse gas, well from our previous slides, we've learned

70
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:52,280
 that it's all three of these methane, water vapor and carbon dioxide with CO2 being the

71
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:57,600
 number one to control.

72
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:02,400
 The presence of carbon dioxide and all the other greenhouse gases in the atmosphere leads

73
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:10,600
 to a phenomenon called the greenhouse effect.

74
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:17,480
 These gases being in our atmosphere trap the energy that comes to earth from our very

75
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:20,200
 own sun.

76
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:29,520
 The sun warms the earth's surface, that heat bounces back up, radiates outward, but all

77
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:36,240
 of it doesn't get to escape back into space, so some of it's reflected back down to earth

78
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:43,280
 by the greenhouse gases.

79
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:50,520
 So we could say the greenhouse gases we have here on earth act like a sort of a atmospheric

80
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:57,000
 blanket, just like you sleeping under a blanket, your body heat warms the blanket and traps

81
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,040
 the heat, so that blanket keeps you warm.

82
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:07,800
 As we get more greenhouse gases accumulating in the atmosphere, it's like having a thicker

83
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:16,400
 blanket, more heat is retained and re-radiated, just like a warmer temperature under a blanket

84
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:18,800
 that you might use yourself.

85
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:25,200
 Well, what if I told you that the greenhouse effect is good for life here on earth?

86
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:31,840
 Well, that actually is true, it's not in itself a dangerous phenomenon, it's necessary for

87
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:35,480
 humans and all other living organisms here on earth.

88
00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:42,960
 If earth's atmosphere did not have some greenhouse gases, too much heat would be lost to space,

89
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:46,840
 and then the earth would be too cold to support life.

90
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:53,320
 The danger imposed by anthropogenic global warming is in the speed of temperature increase,

91
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:57,920
 and thus the changes in weather patterns and sea levels that exceed the ability of many

92
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:00,340
 living organisms to adapt.

93
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:08,000
 So what might this mean for our very own species?

94
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:13,560
 Well remember we have a lot of water here on earth, and bodies of water absorb energy

95
00:09:13,560 --> 00:09:18,400
 and help maintain stable temperatures here on earth.

96
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:23,920
 You'll remember with the hydrogen bonds that water has between the water molecules, it

97
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:28,640
 makes water heat up slowly and resist temperature changes.

98
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:37,320
 This is actually good for life on earth because we don't like drastic temperature changes.

99
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:43,240
 Heat is the total amount of energy associated with the movement of atoms and molecules,

100
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:50,280
 and temperature measures the intensity of heat or how fast the molecules are moving.

101
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:57,640
 Large volumes contain more heat due to greater totals of molecular movements.

102
00:09:57,640 --> 00:10:02,560
 Again the formation of hydrogen bonds between neighboring molecules of water makes it more

103
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:09,040
 cohesive than other liquids, in other words water molecules tend to stick together.

104
00:10:09,040 --> 00:10:13,840
 These same hydrogen bonds also make water resistant to temperature changes.

105
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:19,480
 Even when a large amount of heat is added, that phenomenon occurs because when water

106
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:24,960
 is heated the energy first must disrupt the hydrogen bonds.

107
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:29,940
 Only after enough of the hydrogen bonds have been broken can heat cause individual water

108
00:10:29,940 --> 00:10:35,040
 molecules to move faster, thus increasing their temperature.

109
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:41,720
 When water cools, the hydrogen bonds reform between adjacent water molecules.

110
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:44,720
 This releases heat into the atmosphere.

111
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:50,560
 A body of water can store a large amount of heat from its surroundings while experiencing

112
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:58,760
 only a small increase in temperature and vice versa.

113
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:05,880
 Water's heat, absorbing capacity, has important effects on our Earth's climate.

114
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:11,560
 And after all we have a lot of water on Earth with all of our oceans and lakes and all that

115
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:17,840
 water helps to keep temperatures moderate by absorbing huge amounts of heat radiated

116
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:23,360
 by the sun and then releasing that heat during less sunnier times.

117
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:41,360
 This warms the air and prevents large temperature swings.

118
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:56,360
 [BLANK_AUDIO]

119
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:29,840
 As the temperature continues to rise, individual water molecules can move fast enough to break

120
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:34,980
 free of all hydrogen bonds and then rise up into the air as water vapor.

121
00:12:34,980 --> 00:12:39,960
 And that's the basis for our water cycle here on Earth.

122
00:12:39,960 --> 00:12:47,520
 Water moves from land, oceans, and lakes to clouds, and then back to the Earth's surfaces

123
00:12:47,520 --> 00:12:53,880
 again, as we see in this diagram here.

124
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:59,760
 As the amount of heat trapped near our Earth's surface has increased, well, the water cycle

125
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:00,760
 has changed.

126
00:13:00,760 --> 00:13:02,200
 It's sped up.

127
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:09,120
 The intensification of the cycle leads to more extremes on both ends of the climate spectrum.

128
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:14,500
 In other words, wet places are getting wetter and dry places are getting drier.

129
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:24,000
 The carbon dioxide increase driving this change comes from many different sources.

130
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,680
 First I'll ask this question, "How does your body heat compare to the cool lake where

131
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:31,160
 you swim?"

132
00:13:31,160 --> 00:13:37,280
 Well the answer is B. Remember that heat is the total amount of energy associated with

133
00:13:37,280 --> 00:13:43,520
 the movement of atoms and molecules, while a lake contains more heat because it has a

134
00:13:43,520 --> 00:13:49,200
 greater volume of molecular movement than just your body.

135
00:13:49,200 --> 00:13:58,880
 To help us understand a little bit more about our greenhouse gases like carbon dioxide, let's

136
00:13:58,880 --> 00:14:02,400
 take a look at the flow of carbon on Earth.

137
00:14:02,400 --> 00:14:07,680
 The flow of carbon on Earth is called the carbon cycle.

138
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:13,620
 Remember that the atoms that make up living organisms primarily contain a lot of carbon.

139
00:14:13,620 --> 00:14:20,320
 That's why we call life here on Earth carbon based life or organic life.

140
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:27,040
 Carbon can also be found in the atmosphere as carbon dioxide, it can also be found absorbed

141
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:42,520
 in the water, lakes and our oceans and other bodies of water and even in rocks as well.

142
00:14:42,520 --> 00:14:48,760
 And most of the carbon dioxide in our atmosphere is actually not from natural processes, it

143
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:56,920
 comes from the combustion of fossil fuels, which as you can see when we go back to the

144
00:14:56,920 --> 00:15:03,800
 this picture is shown here with industry emitting carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.

145
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:15,520
 It's also emitted into the atmosphere from Earth through volcanoes as well.

146
00:15:15,520 --> 00:15:23,600
 As part of the carbon cycle, carbon dioxide is absorbed by plants and algae and some bacteria

147
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:26,920
 who are able to perform photosynthesis.

148
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:33,680
 They take in the carbon dioxide and they convert it to sugars or carbohydrates with the use

149
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:36,080
 of sunlight energy.

150
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:41,520
 And then this, the sugars created by photosynthesis are the source of cellular energy

151
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:45,800
 for most organisms here on Earth.

152
00:15:45,800 --> 00:15:53,560
 That carbon is eventually then released by the organisms that consume the carbohydrates

153
00:15:53,560 --> 00:15:59,360
 by, well, breathing for one thing when you breathe out carbon dioxide that goes back

154
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:01,400
 into the atmosphere.

155
00:16:01,400 --> 00:16:08,440
 But you can also release carbon back to Earth when you die as well and decomposers break

156
00:16:08,440 --> 00:16:15,440
 down the carbohydrates in your body and other molecules and then release that back to Earth.

157
00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:24,000
 There are some unconsumed carbohydrates that have been buried for millennia and those

158
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:29,200
 are called fossil fuels.

159
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:34,760
 These are highly concentrated energy sources, lots of carbon and hydrogen and bonds with

160
00:16:34,760 --> 00:16:40,600
 their potential energy in those bonds, just waiting there for us to dig them out and burn

161
00:16:40,600 --> 00:16:44,480
 them as our fuel.

162
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:50,360
 These were produced from unconsumed carbohydrates of ancient buried organisms.

163
00:16:50,360 --> 00:16:54,440
 They're transformed by heat and pressure.

164
00:16:54,440 --> 00:17:00,600
 Examples include petroleum, coal, and natural gas.

165
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:07,120
 Humans now use fossil fuels, especially since the industrial revolution to power homes, vehicles,

166
00:17:07,120 --> 00:17:08,640
 and businesses.

167
00:17:08,640 --> 00:17:16,880
 This releases that stored carbon that's been there for millions of years and then, unfortunately,

168
00:17:16,880 --> 00:17:25,600
 contributes to the accumulation of atmospheric carbon dioxide.

169
00:17:25,600 --> 00:17:29,320
 Human use of fossil fuels is having a measurable effect.

170
00:17:29,320 --> 00:17:35,160
 Increases in carbon dioxide in the atmosphere are well documented by direct measurements

171
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:38,240
 over the past 50 years.

172
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:42,560
 In addition, scientists can analyze fossil air.

173
00:17:42,560 --> 00:17:43,560
 Can you believe it?

174
00:17:43,560 --> 00:17:44,560
 Fossilized air.

175
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:52,280
 But yeah, it's been trapped within ancient ice sheets and then we can use that to determine

176
00:17:52,280 --> 00:17:57,440
 carbon dioxide over longer time spans.

177
00:17:57,440 --> 00:18:05,240
 Such measurement is possible because snow falling on an ice sheet traps the air as snow accumulates

178
00:18:05,240 --> 00:18:12,520
 underlying snow is compressed into ice and the trapped air becomes tiny, ice encased

179
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:14,320
 air bubbles.

180
00:18:14,320 --> 00:18:23,280
 It gives us a way to look back into history and see what the earth's atmosphere was like

181
00:18:23,280 --> 00:18:26,440
 at the time those air bubbles were trapped.

182
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:30,760
 These are called ice cores.

183
00:18:30,760 --> 00:18:36,760
 From these, we were able to see that current carbon dioxide levels are the highest level

184
00:18:36,760 --> 00:18:45,160
 in over 400,000 years and we know temperature and carbon dioxide levels are correlated.

185
00:18:45,160 --> 00:18:51,960
 First, I want to go back to this graph as well as we can see the average concentration

186
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:56,600
 of carbon dioxide steadily increasing over time.

187
00:18:56,600 --> 00:19:04,320
 Here, carbon dioxide concentration is measured in parts per million.

188
00:19:04,320 --> 00:19:10,680
 And this graph is only from 1960 to 2017.

189
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:15,920
 Imagine what the graph would look like over the last 400,000 years.

190
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:23,960
 But this is well documented actual data and evidence.

191
00:19:23,960 --> 00:19:32,740
 Again, temperature and carbon dioxide levels are correlated and here we can see that.

192
00:19:32,740 --> 00:19:39,200
 Here we see records of temperature and atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration from Antarctic

193
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:40,880
 ice cores.

194
00:19:40,880 --> 00:19:46,800
 This data indicates that increases in carbon dioxide levels are correlated with higher

195
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:47,800
 temperatures.

196
00:19:47,800 --> 00:19:54,740
 So, when carbon dioxide levels and the atmosphere are high, well, take a look at what happens

197
00:19:54,740 --> 00:19:56,040
 to the temperature.

198
00:19:56,040 --> 00:19:59,000
 The temperature also goes up.

199
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:06,000
 Take a look at the present one right here.

200
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:14,320
 Carbon dioxide concentration is way up here and what's going to happen to our temperature.

201
00:20:14,320 --> 00:20:17,120
 If we look in the past, look at the blue line.

202
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:27,760
 This is what's happened every time our carbon dioxide levels have gotten high.

203
00:20:27,760 --> 00:20:32,920
 There's a video embedded into this slide show called the carbon cycle.

204
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:38,480
 If you're unable to play it over your slide show, you can also click on this YouTube video

205
00:20:38,480 --> 00:20:53,400
 here or just do a search on YouTube for carbon cycle and there's a plethora of videos available.

206
00:20:53,400 --> 00:20:59,760
 Modern plants and certain microorganisms do as their ancient predecessors did, they absorb

207
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:04,800
 carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and convert it to carbohydrates.

208
00:21:04,800 --> 00:21:10,480
 Is it possible that we could depend on these modern organisms to reduce the amount of greenhouse

209
00:21:10,480 --> 00:21:16,760
 gases released by fossil fuel burning and thus the threat posed by global climate change?

210
00:21:16,760 --> 00:21:24,040
 Well, photosynthesis is the process that traps light energy from the sun and uses it to convert

211
00:21:24,040 --> 00:21:28,880
 carbon dioxide and water into sugars or carbohydrates.

212
00:21:28,880 --> 00:21:35,240
 In other words, photosynthesis transforms solar energy into the chemical energy required

213
00:21:35,240 --> 00:21:40,000
 by all living things here on Earth.

214
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:46,680
 In the picture below, we see a chloroplasts, which is an organelle that all photosynthetic

215
00:21:46,680 --> 00:21:51,680
 organisms have in order to do these reactions.

216
00:21:51,680 --> 00:22:03,760
 Plants, some protists like algae and some bacteria like cyanobacteria are able to do this.

217
00:22:03,760 --> 00:22:12,640
 A chloroplasts is like an envelope, it has an inner and outer membrane to enclose it.

218
00:22:12,640 --> 00:22:18,760
 Some other parts are the stroma, which is the fluid-filled compartment.

219
00:22:18,760 --> 00:22:24,480
 This holds a lot of enzymes or proteins for doing the process of photosynthesis.

220
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:27,480
 It's like a thick fluid area.

221
00:22:27,480 --> 00:22:32,120
 Then there's these little stacks of disks called the phylicoids.

222
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:37,400
 These are little structures that have membranes of their own that are stacked in piles and

223
00:22:37,400 --> 00:22:45,000
 again increase the surface area for areas to do photosynthesis reactions.

224
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:52,440
 These phylicoids also hold chlorophyll, which is different pigment molecules.

225
00:22:52,440 --> 00:22:56,360
 There's more than one type of chlorophyll, but there are different pigment molecules

226
00:22:56,360 --> 00:22:58,640
 made of protein.

227
00:22:58,640 --> 00:23:04,600
 They cover the surface of the phylicoid disk and they absorb light energy from the sun.

228
00:23:04,600 --> 00:23:07,120
 That's their job.

229
00:23:07,120 --> 00:23:16,020
 Some of them are more made to absorb the electromagnetic spectrum parts that are purple

230
00:23:16,020 --> 00:23:19,840
 and blue that we would see as visible light.

231
00:23:19,840 --> 00:23:26,400
 Others, red and oranges, but you'll notice that most of these photosynthetic organisms

232
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:34,160
